(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-prof-shah-style-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Prof. Shah Style Integration Set 2 — Anatomy Exam 4","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which muscle abducts the arm at the shoulder and is the large muscle capping the shoulder joint (deep to which the supraspinatus lies)?","choices":["Pectoralis major","Deltoid","Latissimus dorsi","Coracobrachialis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The deltoid caps the shoulder and abducts the arm; the supraspinatus, which also abducts, lies deep to it. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which muscle produces flexion, adduction, AND medial rotation of the arm at the shoulder from its origin on the clavicle and sternum?","choices":["Pectoralis major","Deltoid","Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pectoralis major flexes, adducts, and medially rotates the arm and arises from the clavicle and sternum. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which muscle supinates the forearm and lies in the posterior/deep forearm alongside the anconeus?","choices":["Pronator teres","Pronator quadratus","Supinator","Brachioradialis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The supinator supinates the forearm and is a deep posterior forearm muscle grouped with the anconeus. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which iliopsoas-group muscle is described as a MAJOR flexor of the hip?","choices":["Piriformis","Psoas major","Quadratus femoris","Gluteus medius"],"answer":1,"exp":"The psoas major is described as a major flexor of the hip within the iliopsoas group. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which muscle allows crossing of the lower leg and flexes both the knee and the hip, coursing obliquely across the anterior thigh?","choices":["Rectus femoris","Vastus lateralis","Biceps femoris","Sartorius"],"answer":3,"exp":"The sartorius crosses the thigh obliquely, flexes both the knee and the hip, and lets you cross the lower leg. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which quadriceps muscle both extends the lower leg AND flexes the hip because it crosses two joints?","choices":["Rectus femoris","Vastus lateralis","Vastus medialis","Vastus intermedius"],"answer":0,"exp":"Of the quadriceps, only the rectus femoris crosses the hip as well as the knee, so it extends the lower leg and flexes the hip. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which two muscles occupy the LATERAL compartment of the leg and evert the foot?","choices":["Gastrocnemius and soleus","Tibialis anterior and posterior","Plantaris and soleus","Fibularis (peroneus) longus and brevis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fibularis (peroneus) longus and brevis form the lateral compartment of the leg and evert the foot. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"Which lateral-rotator-group muscle laterally rotates and abducts the hip and exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic notch?","choices":["Piriformis","Adductor longus","Pectineus","Iliacus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The piriformis laterally rotates and abducts the hip and is the lateral-rotator-group muscle passing through the greater sciatic notch. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which facial muscle compresses the cheeks (as in blowing or keeping food between the teeth) and is innervated by the facial nerve (CN VII)?","choices":["Masseter","Temporalis","Buccinator","Platysma"],"answer":2,"exp":"The buccinator compresses the cheeks and, like all facial expression muscles, is innervated by the facial nerve (CN VII). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which muscle elevates the upper eyelid, is innervated by cranial nerve III, and produces ptosis (drooping lid) when it fails?","choices":["Orbicularis oculi","Levator palpebrae superioris","Superior rectus","Corrugator supercilii"],"answer":1,"exp":"Levator palpebrae superioris elevates the upper eyelid via the oculomotor nerve (CN III); its dysfunction causes ptosis. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which tongue muscle protrudes the tongue (moving it out and down) and is innervated by the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)?","choices":["Palatoglossus","Hyoglossus","Styloglossus","Genioglossus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Genioglossus moves the tongue out and down (protrusion) and is supplied by the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII). — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which muscle of mastication inserts on the angle and lateral surface of the mandible?","choices":["Masseter","Temporalis","Medial pterygoid","Lateral pterygoid"],"answer":0,"exp":"The masseter inserts on the angle and lateral surface of the mandible. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which group of cervical muscles elevates the ribs to assist inspiration?","choices":["The scalene muscles","The suprahyoid muscles","The infrahyoid strap muscles","The erector spinae"],"answer":0,"exp":"All of the scalene muscles (anterior, middle, posterior) elevate the ribs during inhalation. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which deep intrinsic back muscle extends the vertebral column and rotates the body toward the side OPPOSITE the muscle?","choices":["Interspinales","Multifidus","Intertransversarii","Iliocostalis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Multifidus (like the rotatores) extends the vertebral column and rotates the trunk toward the opposite side. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which muscle laterally flexes the vertebral column and is innervated by thoracic and lumbar spinal nerves, spanning between the iliac crest and the 12th rib / lumbar transverse processes?","choices":["Longus capitis","Longus colli","Multifidus","Quadratus lumborum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Quadratus lumborum laterally flexes the vertebral column and is supplied by thoracic and lumbar spinal nerves. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which abdominal muscle ONLY compresses the abdomen (without depressing ribs or laterally flexing the trunk), forming the deepest of the three flat abdominal layers?","choices":["External oblique","Internal oblique","Transversus abdominis","Rectus abdominis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Transversus abdominis is the deepest flat abdominal muscle and only compresses the abdomen. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which forearm bone is lateral to its partner, has a head that pivots on the capitulum of the humerus, and bears the radial tuberosity proximally?","choices":["Ulna","Radius","Humerus","Scaphoid"],"answer":1,"exp":"The radius is lateral to the ulna; its head pivots on the capitulum of the humerus and it carries the radial tuberosity. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which leg bone is medial to its partner and bears the tibial tuberosity, the intercondylar eminence, and the medial malleolus?","choices":["Fibula","Femur","Talus","Tibia"],"answer":3,"exp":"The tibia is medial to the fibula and carries the tibial tuberosity, intercondylar eminence, and medial malleolus. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which end of the clavicle is the medial end that articulates with the sternum?","choices":["Sternal end","Acromial end","Conoid tubercle","Costal tuberosity"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sternal end is the medial end of the clavicle (it articulates with the sternum); the acromial end is lateral. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which set lists the four DISTAL carpal bones?","choices":["Scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform","Scaphoid, trapezoid, capitate, hamate","Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate","Trapezium, lunate, capitate, pisiform"],"answer":2,"exp":"The distal carpal row is trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate — the second half of 'Sally left the party to take Cathy home.' — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which pelvic feature best distinguishes a female pelvis, being greater than 100 degrees in females and less than 90 degrees in males?","choices":["The obturator foramen","The acetabulum","The pubic angle","The greater sciatic notch"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pubic angle is greater than 100 degrees in females and less than 90 degrees in males, an adaptation for childbearing. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which medial hip-bone surface articulates with the sacrum at the sacroiliac joint?","choices":["The iliac fossa","The auricular surface","The arcuate line","The obturator groove"],"answer":1,"exp":"The auricular surface of the ilium articulates with the sacrum to form the sacroiliac joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which cranial bone bears the crista galli, the cribriform plate, the perpendicular plate, and the superior and middle nasal conchae?","choices":["Sphenoid","Ethmoid","Vomer","Frontal bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ethmoid has the crista galli, cribriform plate, perpendicular plate, and superior and middle nasal conchae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which bone contains the foramen magnum, the occipital condyles, and the superior and inferior nuchal lines?","choices":["Temporal bone","Parietal bone","Occipital bone","Sphenoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"The occipital bone bears the foramen magnum, the occipital condyles (which articulate with C1), and the superior and inferior nuchal lines. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which opening transmits the oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV), ophthalmic (V1), and abducens (VI) nerves?","choices":["Optic canal","Superior orbital fissure","Foramen ovale","Jugular foramen"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior orbital fissure transmits cranial nerves III, IV, V1, and VI. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which vertebra has a dens (odontoid process) held by the transverse ligament and permits the 'no' rotational motion of the head?","choices":["Atlas (C1)","Vertebra prominens (C7)","Axis (C2)","Lumbar vertebra (L3)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The axis (C2) has the dens, which the transverse ligament binds to the atlas, allowing the 'no' rotation of the head. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which bone does NOT articulate with any other bone (making it extremely mobile) and consists of a body with greater and lesser horns?","choices":["Vomer","Mandible","Sphenoid","Hyoid bone"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hyoid bone articulates with no other bone (it is suspended by the stylohyoid ligaments) and has a body with greater and lesser horns. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which part of the sternum is the superior portion that bears the jugular notch and articulates with the clavicle?","choices":["Manubrium","Body","Xiphoid process","Costal cartilage"],"answer":0,"exp":"The manubrium is the superior part of the sternum; it carries the jugular notch and articulates with the clavicle. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which distinctive feature do ALL cervical vertebrae share (with bifid spinous processes in most, except C7)?","choices":["Costal facets for ribs","Very large vertebral bodies","Fusion into a single bone","Transverse foramina"],"answer":3,"exp":"All cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina, and their spinous processes are bifid except for C7. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which opening transmits the mandibular nerve (V3) as it leaves the middle cranial fossa?","choices":["Foramen ovale","Optic canal","Superior orbital fissure","Jugular foramen"],"answer":0,"exp":"The foramen ovale of the sphenoid transmits the mandibular nerve (V3). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"}]};
